    },
    {
      "component_id": "c_033",
      "class": "android.widget.TextView",
      "text": "How accurate is the speedometer?",
      "content_desc": "",
      "resource_id": "",
      "clickable": false,
      "enabled": true,
      "focusable": false,
      "bounds": [
        0,
        3430,
        870,
        3506
      ]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "c_034",
      "class": "android.widget.TextView",
      "text": "The speedometer is as accurate as your phone's GPS receiver. Therefore a G1's GPS receiver will not perform as well as a more recent Android device. Additionally, your GPS accuracy can be affected by satellite positions, noise in the radio signal, atmospheric conditions, and large barriers (such as buildings or mountains) to the signal. These conditions can produce GPS accuracy errors up to 30 meters. In order to alleviate these accuracy errors, the application will by default ignore speeds over 150 mph to prevent erroneous speed spikes (this can be disabled in the settings). The application will also ignore GPS data if the accuracy reported by the GPS is not at least 200 meters accurate (this value can be adjusted in the settings).",
      "content_desc": "",
      "resource_id": "",
      "clickable": false,
      "enabled": true,
      "focusable": false,
      "bounds": [
        0,
        3541,
        1440,
        4234
      ]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "c_035",
      "class": "android.widget.TextView",
      "text": "What do the GPS statuses mean?",
      "content_desc": "",
      "resource_id": "",
      "clickable": false,
      "enabled": true,
      "focusable": false,
      "bounds": [
        0,
        4269,
        834,
        4345
      ]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "c_036",
      "class": "android.widget.TextView",
      "text": "When your phone is 'Searching for GPS' then a GPS signal has not yet been acquired. When it finds a GPS lock, then status will change to 'GPS Found' and then 'GPS Locked' meaning that the application is ready to use GPS features (speedometer, weather, location, etc.). If the GPS is experiencing low accuracy issues, then the status will change to 'GPS Low Accuracy'. Beside of the GPS status description is a count of the number of satellites that are currently being used to determine your signal.",
      "content_desc": "",
      "resource_id": "",
      "clickable": false,
      "enabled": true,
      "focusable": false,
      "bounds": [
        0,
        4380,
        1440,
        4845
      ]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "c_037",
      "class": "android.widget.TextView",
      "text": "How do the speed alerts work?",
